WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.600
Vsauce! Kevin here -- with a math&nbsp;
problem. Or a moral problem. Or BOTH.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:14.320
In 1972, two men were convicted of cashing&nbsp;
fraudulent checks, one for $58.40 and the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:24.000
for $35.20. Neither had a criminal history.&nbsp;
One got 30 days -- the other got 15 years.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:30.720
In the Arizona v. Marlow murder case, the&nbsp;
defendant was sentenced to death -- and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.960
co-defendant who also participated&nbsp;
in the killing got just 4 years.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.000
And a 2016 working paper from the&nbsp;
National Bureau of Economic Research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:49.040
showed that judges in Louisiana gave harsher&nbsp;
sentences to juvenile offenders on the Mondays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.200
after the LSU Tigers football team lost a game.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.840
What makes criminal sentencing so unfair?

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.520
At the most basic level, justice is&nbsp;
‘giving people what they deserve.’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:15.520
Part of that might be punitive -- if you’ve&nbsp;
harmed someone else or society in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:22.320
maybe you deserve to be punished. Part might be&nbsp;
protection -- that society shouldn’t be exposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:30.640
to continual threats of harm. Or should sentences&nbsp;
be purely about restitution and rehabilitation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:37.200
with a restorative focus on healing the&nbsp;
victim and improving the offender? If so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:44.400
should a criminal only serve as much time as it&nbsp;
takes to make sure it won’t happen ever again?

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:52.800
A just sentence has to find a balance between&nbsp;
all these valid philosophical questions -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.640
outcomes can vary tremendously&nbsp;
from judge to judge. TREMENDOUSLY.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:06.960
In 1973, the sentence in the United States for&nbsp;
a federal charge of kidnapping was “any term of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:14.480
years to life.” You could get probation or you&nbsp;
could get 25 years -- depending on the judge.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:21.360
Federal judge Marvin Frankel called the potential&nbsp;
for sentencing disparities, “... unthinkable in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.760
government of laws, not men.” His book&nbsp;
“Law Without Order” exposed shocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:34.560
variations in federal sentencing as a result of&nbsp;
a complex system in which hundreds of years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:40.720
ever-evolving law can determine your guilt,&nbsp;
while a robed public official who’s in a bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:48.000
mood because they’ve got heartburn can make you&nbsp;
pay whatever price they want in that moment.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.840
Frankel highlighted, “... the almost&nbsp;
wholly unchecked and sweeping powers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.400
we give to judges in the fashioning&nbsp;
of sentences,” and he called them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:02.480
“terrifying and intolerable for a society&nbsp;
that professes devotion to the rule of law.”

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:09.520
But the math-based cure was worse&nbsp;
than the disease of human variability.

