00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.360
Okay. Here’s the thing.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:17.760
Sentencing is hard. Look. Let’s try&nbsp;
to solve sentencing for property crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:24.320
right now: a judge calculates the amount of&nbsp;
labor it takes the crime victim to get a thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:30.960
and a thief serves exactly that amount of time&nbsp;
as punishment for stealing it. So let's say you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:39.120
make $20 an hour, someone steals your $1,000&nbsp;
TV. So, they'd serve the 50 hours of labor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:46.240
they took from you. The sentence fits the harm&nbsp;
done in the crime, and everyone’s sentence would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:53.040
be calculated without prejudice. That&nbsp;
sounds perfectly fair, right? WRONG.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.840
We’ve just incentivized everyone&nbsp;
to go steal Elon Musk’s car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:04.880
because your punishment would only be the&nbsp;
2 minutes of labor it took him to buy it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:10.960
Every incarnation of justice&nbsp;
has unintended consequences.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.960
But you can eliminate statistical outliers.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:21.920
The Sentencing Reform Act passed in 1984 as&nbsp;
a result of Frankel’s advocacy for a fairer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:27.760
more consistent justice system -- and both&nbsp;
Republicans and Dem ocrats supported its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:34.080
introduction of objectivity: putting crimes&nbsp;
through a complex algorithm to determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:40.880
sentences, with mandatory minimums and&nbsp;
maximums that lessened the impact of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:49.120
outright bias and just plain philosophical&nbsp;
disagreement among judges. Rehabilitation was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:57.280
abandoned; simple punishment was the goal. And&nbsp;
what’s more objective than numbers and formulas?

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.200
It worked like this.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:07.760
A crime was assigned one of 43 levels -- so,&nbsp;
trespassing is Level 4, kidnapping is Level 32.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:15.200
Then levels could be added or subtracted based on&nbsp;
the specifics of a crime. A Level 20 robbery would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:23.120
become a Level 25 if a firearm was brandished,&nbsp;
and a Level 27 if it was actually fired. If the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:30.080
offender was a lesser participant in the crime, 4&nbsp;
levels would be taken away down to Level 23. Did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:37.120
they accept responsibility? 2 more levels gone.&nbsp;
1 level gone for pleading guilty. Back to level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:44.560
20! Then that number was put into a 6-category&nbsp;
criminal history table to calculate the range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:53.440
sentencing: for a Level 20 offense by someone with&nbsp;
a Category III criminal history? 41 to 51 months.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:58.320
But even determining levels&nbsp;
ended up having problems.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:05.760
In the 1991 case Chapman v. United States, the&nbsp;
court decided that the medium in which a drug was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:12.960
transmitted would count as the weight of the drug&nbsp;
being factored into a dealer’s sentence. The case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:22.640
involved LSD -- selling 100 doses in sugar cubes&nbsp;
came with a sentence of 188 to 235 months. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:31.360
dealer with 100 doses in paper would get 63 to&nbsp;
78 months because paper weighed so much less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:40.560
sugar cubes. 100 doses of pure, unadulterated&nbsp;
LSD with no mixture? Only 10 to 16 months.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:46.960
ONE SINGLE DOSE OF LSD IN THIS GALLON OF&nbsp;
CHOCCY MILK WOULD GET ME SENTENCED FOR ABOUT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:57.840
4 KILOS OF SCHEDULE 1 DRUGS AND SERVE OVER&nbsp;
20 YEARS IN PRISON AS A FIRST TIME OFFENDER.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.920
NONE OF THIS MAKES ANY SENSE.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.800
An appeals judge said, "To base&nbsp;
punishment on the weight of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:14.640
carrier medium makes about as much sense as&nbsp;
basing punishment on the weight of the defendant.”

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:22.720
Running crimes through rubrics and point systems&nbsp;
to determine arbitrary numerical values divorced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:29.280
sentences from the unique aspects of the&nbsp;
crime and its victims, and the characteristics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:37.040
of the offender. It also totally nullifies a&nbsp;
judge’s lifetime of professional expertise.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:40.960
But mandatory federal sentencing guidelines…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:48.160
actually DID solve problems. A study by the&nbsp;
US Sentencing Commission found that in 1986,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:57.840
sentences varied by about 17% -- and just 7 years&nbsp;
later that variation was cut to 11%. Mandated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:06.400
sentences cut down the influence of judges’ whims&nbsp;
-- against some defendants and in favor of others.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:08.480
But it didn’t matter.

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:14.000
The Supreme Court struck down&nbsp;
federal sentencing mandates in 2005&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:21.120
citing a violation of the 6th Amendment. Just 3&nbsp;
years after Marvin Frankel’s death, sentencing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:28.640
mandates were now just suggested guidelines --&nbsp;
and the variations in sentencing came right back.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:36.640
So, in some ways the algorithm&nbsp;
worked. It reduced extreme sentences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:42.000
some systemic bias, statistical&nbsp;
noise, and standard deviations.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:52.400
And it also failed badly with its robotic,&nbsp;
arbitrary, and sometimes nonsensical results. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.960 --> 00:10:00.880
flatout made some things much worse. Like messed&nbsp;
up mandatory minimum and determinate sentences.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:07.760
Judges were happy to get their professional&nbsp;
discretion back -- 75% preferred the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:15.920
system of using the guidelines as an advisory&nbsp;
tool while only 3% preferred pure mandates.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:23.520
But how does one human mind discern&nbsp;
exactly the right amount of punishment?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:28.800
If you steal something, why isn’t it&nbsp;
enough just to pay the person back?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:37.040
How fair is it that someone can take a life,&nbsp;
yet only pay with 10 or 12 years of their own?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:42.800
To what exact degree does a first time offender&nbsp;
differ from a second time offender from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:49.120
a tenth-time offender? Do we ever&nbsp;
just give up on rehabilitation?

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:54.160
Is a criminal responsible for the&nbsp;
harm he does to future generations?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.800 --> 00:11:02.240
Does an eye for an eye mean severe retribution?&nbsp;
Or does it actually place a limit on a fitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:08.960
punishment? Are there any crimes we should&nbsp;
just... accept? Should senile elderly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:16.000
people be responsible for the crimes of their&nbsp;
youth? Should we even have victimless crime?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:23.200
Is the possibility of injustice the price we&nbsp;
have to pay for a chance at perfect justice?

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:31.440
Are we trying to punish criminals? Rehabilitate&nbsp;
them? Separate them from society? A combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:38.320
all three? If so – what’s the proper balance&nbsp;
of that? And how do we reduce conscious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:45.680
bias, unconscious variability and noise at a&nbsp;
national level that still allows for the nuance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:52.400
of individual circumstances to be addressed&nbsp;
fairly – city, state, and country-wide?

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:58.080
What’s the algorithm to figure out all of that?

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:02.880
What’s the algorithm… for morality?

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:09.840
What’s the algorithm… for justice?

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:23.840
And as always, thanks for watching.

